![Photo by Frederick Tubiermont on Unsplash](cover.webp)

Ideas sell for a penny a million. How can we make our idea become
reality? Or at least get a positive experience out of it.

Do you want to learn a new programming language? Start a new hobby?
Invent a new JavaScript framework? Or some other crazy idea?

Great — the concept is in your mind and there is a motivation to
begin. But before you throw all your strength at it, give it a
maceration process.

## Maceration

It is a technique normally used in the culinary arts to extract the
flavors of an element. Usually with water or another liquid that
separates the solid from its properties and flavors.

Simply put: if you want to build a personal project, learn a new
skill or technology, start a business, or just bring any idea to
life — you must write.

Write the details of the idea in a notebook, a sheet, a blog or
similar. With freehand drawings if possible. Try to use a pencil
rather than typing, because words flow much better through the
hand.

Detail what you want to do and the steps to follow. Seeking the
greatest possible clarity about the idea's details. The pros and
cons of doing it. The associated risks, the time and money needed.
Who can help you, what you must know to pull it off.

When you feel the idea and all its angles are clear (this process
usually takes an hour or more, depending on the idea), then store it
away.

Now the maceration begins. Keep the paper for at least a month, and
after that time revisit the idea. Maybe it is not as good as it
seemed, or you came up with a simpler way to solve the problem.

If after the maceration time the idea still feels relevant and
worthy of your time, then take action. Execute the steps and give it
all your energy so the idea sees the light.

My uncle had a saying: “With enough patience, even the ant managed
to eat the elephant”.
